{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"mutex-locks","title":"Mutexes, Locks & Deadlock","group":"Concurrency","order":71,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A <strong>data race</strong> — two threads touching the same memory, at least one writing, without synchronisation — is undefined behaviour. A mutex makes a critical section run in one thread at a time.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock\"></i>A race, and the fix</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;mutex&gt;\n#include &lt;thread&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    long long counter = 0;\n    std::mutex m;\n    {\n        std::vector&lt;std::jthread&gt; threads;\n        for (int t = 0; t &lt; 4; ++t)\n            threads.emplace_back([&amp;] {\n                for (int i = 0; i &lt; 100'000; ++i) {\n                    std::lock_guard&lt;std::mutex&gt; lock(m);   // locks now, unlocks at end of scope\n                    ++counter;                             // without the lock: lost updates\n                }\n            });\n    }\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; counter &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>400000</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Why the lock</div><p><code>++counter</code> is read-modify-write: two threads can both read 5 and both write 6. Without the mutex the result is typically less than 400000 — and formally the program has undefined behaviour.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-key\"></i>Lock types</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>RAII lock</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>std::lock_guard&lt;M&gt;</code></td><td>Simplest: lock for the whole scope</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::unique_lock&lt;M&gt;</code></td><td>Can unlock/relock, defer, time out, be moved; required by condition variables</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::scoped_lock&lt;M1, M2…&gt;</code> (C++17)</td><td>Lock several mutexes at once <em>without deadlock</em></td></tr><tr><td><code>std::shared_lock&lt;std::shared_mutex&gt;</code></td><td>Many readers or one writer</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock-x\"></i>Deadlock</h3>\n<p>Thread A locks <code>m1</code> then waits for <code>m2</code>; thread B locks <code>m2</code> then waits for <code>m1</code> — both wait forever. Prevent it by always acquiring locks in the same global order, or by locking them together with <code>std::scoped_lock</code>.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;mutex&gt;\n#include &lt;thread&gt;\n\nstruct Account {\n    int balance;\n    std::mutex m;\n};\n\nvoid transfer(Account&amp; from, Account&amp; to, int amount) {\n    std::scoped_lock lock(from.m, to.m);       // deadlock-free, whatever order callers use\n    from.balance -= amount;\n    to.balance += amount;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    Account a{1000, {}}, b{1000, {}};\n    {\n        std::jthread t1([&amp;] { for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1000; ++i) transfer(a, b, 1); });\n        std::jthread t2([&amp;] { for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1000; ++i) transfer(b, a, 2); });\n    }\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; a.balance &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; b.balance &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; a.balance + b.balance &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2000 0 2000</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-book\"></i>Readers–writer lock</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;map&gt;\n#include &lt;mutex&gt;\n#include &lt;shared_mutex&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;thread&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nclass Config {\n    mutable std::shared_mutex m_;\n    std::map&lt;std::string, int&gt; values_;\npublic:\n    int get(const std::string&amp; k) const {\n        std::shared_lock lock(m_);             // many readers at once\n        auto it = values_.find(k);\n        return it == values_.end() ? 0 : it-&gt;second;\n    }\n    void set(const std::string&amp; k, int v) {\n        std::unique_lock lock(m_);             // exclusive writer\n        values_[k] = v;\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Config cfg;\n    cfg.set(\"threads\", 4);\n    std::vector&lt;std::jthread&gt; readers;\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; seen(4);\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; ++i) readers.emplace_back([&amp;, i] { seen[i] = cfg.get(\"threads\"); });\n    readers.clear();                           // join all\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; seen[0] + seen[1] + seen[2] + seen[3] &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>16</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Keep critical sections short; never call unknown code (callbacks, virtual functions) while holding a lock; use RAII locks only.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Calling <code>m.lock()</code> / <code>m.unlock()</code> by hand — an exception or early return leaves the mutex locked forever.</p></div></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What are the four conditions for deadlock?</summary><div><p>Mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption and circular wait (Coffman conditions). Breaking any one prevents deadlock; in practice you break <em>circular wait</em> with a consistent lock order or by acquiring all locks atomically (<code>std::scoped_lock</code>/<code>std::lock</code>).</p></div></details></section>"}